[Music]  despite its volatility and serious  environmental concerns cryptocurrency  mining is ramping up at a breakneck pace  causing the network to inevitably grow  worldwide  in april of 2021 prominent members of  the community created the crypto climate  accord  inspired by the paris climate accord  their stated goal is to decarbonize  bitcoin mining and other cryptocurrency  energy consumption  [Music]  as signatories of the accord new york  city's bitdigital is doing their part to  practice what they preach while keeping  their eye on the bottom line we're bit  digital we're a bitcoin mining company  and we are  sitting in our new office just off union  square in new york city  reusing sustainable energy sources is  clearly the right thing to do from the  perspective of the planet  and i'm an environmentalist at heart so  that's something i personally care about  in terms of our bottom line it's  important because it's something our  investors care about increasingly  institutional investors are very focused  on compliance with esg goals and we're a  publicly traded company so that's  something we have to be  very thoughtful about  it is an energy-intensive industry  and so it's incumbent on us as a company  and others in the community  to find ways to source that energy that  aren't going to  add to the carbon footprint  so i actually graduated with my master's  degree in civil and environmental  engineering from carnegie mellon  university it's actually my first time  applying what i've learned from school  in real life yeah we have about 46 000  miners across the globe and we estimate  about 55  on renewable or low carbon energy and  we're setting targets to increase them  percentage  but to understand how we're gonna have  to leave their manhattan we work office  and head to a more remote corner of the  country  these co-locations as they're called  service bitcoin mining and other  cryptocurrency computing needs  so right now we're in kearney nebraska  and we're standing on a bitcoin mining  facility it's running about 60 megawatts  of power  and there's about 20 000 individual  bitcoin mining computers in this  facility  can't hear a damn thing in here  we have about 5 000 of these so about a  quarter of these are ours  so those solar panels on the in the next  field that's a part of the generation  capacity that's ultimately powering this  facility right yeah correct so that is  actually the largest solar field in all  of nebraska so 65  of the electricity that comes into our  facility here at compute north  is carbon free  just in the last you know several weeks  to several months though the the demand  has certainly outstripped our ability to  uh supply and to scale at the level that  they need we have had to turn clients  away  [Music]  even though the the scale and the size  of the bitcoin network is is getting  massive i think the easiest thing to  attack is the energy and the the amount  and usage  candidly i could say the same thing for  facebook i'm not a fan of the social  media network i'm not a fan of my  neighbors cat photos i mean they also  use a tremendous amount of energy  i think bitcoin is very different in  that what it is energy intensive it also  can go to places where that energy would  otherwise not be put to use there's more  energy not being used at all and simply  goes to waste than bitcoin even uses by  a factor of 20.  [Music]  this is true primarily because of a  concept called stranded energy which is  essentially power that's generated but  due to grid limitations can't be used  and ultimately goes to waste  crypto miners are now flocking to  co-location sites to make use of this  stranded energy and stranded energy by  its nature is in remote places  this bitcoin mining it is an economic  animal and it's going to seek the lowest  economic cost power it can find that is  often stranded renewables that don't  have any other users available because  nature puts renewable energy wherever  nature chose to put it computers and  bitcoin miners don't care where they  live you can literally put them anywhere  in the world west texas has abundant  wind resources  northern quebec there are miners  operating up there on the hydropower  that's abundant there solar farms  sitting in the middle of a desert and so  we've brought the computational demand  to where the renewable power is located  by bit digital's own estimate however 45  percent of the power their machines draw  remains from non-renewables like oil and  gas to mitigate the environmental  effects of these less carbon neutral  energy sources they've come up with some  innovative solutions i have a  partnership in alberta canada with a  company that develops mining sites that  sit on top of dormant natural gas wells  and they are venting natural gas into  the atmosphere instead of venting that  natural gas which is mostly methane into  the atmosphere  it's being captured and repurposed to  create energy to run bitcoin mining and  by doing that and converting it into  fuel  it actually reduces the emissions the  greenhouse gas emissions of that gas  still eco-conscious miners do need to go  even further in order to curb their  power consumption however mining  machines don't need to run 24 hours a  day seven days a week like other more  critical networks  and miners like bit digital have  agreements with their power providers to  curtail usage during peak demand when  there is grid needs when there's a  better use for the energy when there's a  great emergency we can and we do shut  down and that's a very key part of our  whole mantra of being not just a good  citizen locally but being a good citizen  to the energy into the power networks  but without worldwide enforceable  regulations efforts like this could very  well be in vain  if mining remains this lucrative there  will always be people that put profit  over planet which gives the more  conscientious miners all the more reason  to get machines out of the regions of  the world with less regulations and into  areas with better transparency and  accountability  about a mile away there's a warehouse  that's full of a bunch that we recently  flew over from china  [Music]  and you know frankly uh it's not great  to see them sitting here in boxes  because they could be plugged in and  making money and i want to get them out  and making some money as quickly as  possible  all the miners as you see today are  actually manufactured  and designed by chinese companies  and going from there it's a process of  distributing all those miners across the  globe to make it more you know  decentralized and that's exactly what we  are doing here we're as a minor to  support the bitcoin network depending on  the model and the exact moment you buy  them are going to cost 10 to 15 000 a  piece  and um  it's kind of like a souped-up ps5  you plug it in and it starts making this  commodity we know as bitcoin  bitcoin has  had a bit of a democratizing effect  people can actually create pretty  attractive returns for themselves  without having a whole lot of money to  put to work  you know that was enabled by bitcoin and  now it's spread to a bigger ecosystem  to bitcoin devoutees that ecosystem as  energy intensive as it may be has the  potential to be less harmful to the  environment than other more traditional  forms of stored value  what's really striking about this moment  is  for the first time really in human  history  we've got a truly global  asset class  that is in many ways like gold it's a  store of value with a fixed supply and  you can create it with the sun  i'd rather see a bunch of robots using  energy from the sun  to mint bitcoins versus gold  using human beings and burning fossil  fuels and stripping open mountaintops to  mine gold  [Music]  [Laughter]  [Music]  you

